er, and occasionally flour. The mixture is heated until its temperature reaches the hard crack stage of 149 to 154 °C (300 to 310 °F). While being prepared, toffee is sometimes mixed with nuts or raisins."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-toffee"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toffee"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Mesir macunu","dish/mesir-macunu"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f3/Thorntonstoffee.jpg/500px-Thorntonstoffee.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Stratford490","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thorntonstoffee.jpg"}},"dish/pekasam":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pekasam","n":"Pekasam","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Pekasam, Pakasam or Bekasam is a Malay term for fermented food, more precisely fermented fish product. In Malay and Banjar cookery, pekasam usually refers to freshwater fish fermented with salt, palm sugar, toasted rice grains and pieces of asam gelugur."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pekasam"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pekasam"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Freshwater fish","ingredient/freshwater-fish"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam peda